& [UserIntent] preserves the hashed attention reentry objective
  + the user describes an executable attention frame with stack and variables whose trajectory can be hashed and reentered when one frame is insufficient
  + the user expects a hard-coded attention procedure to reduce memory demand to identifying inputs outputs conditions and transforms
  + the user describes session-scoped procedures and a main that calls subroutines across several passes
  + the user wants Cantor callable as a local inference coprocessor rather than requiring a modified llama.cpp

& [MeasuredPredecessor] narrows the useful session
  + exact measurement shows quota selection removes more structured traffic than a context-only handle
  + admitted context is 18653 bytes while dynamic checkpoints reach 44890 bytes in the fine schedule
  + a useful handle must therefore retain both exact context and current checkpoint rather than context alone
  + complete current tool metadata is 56243 bytes so a compact model-facing command schema must not recursively embed all core forms

& [SelectedSession] is volatile content-bound and inspectable
  + OPEN admits one exact typed context encoded as a bounded JSON string creates the genesis checkpoint and returns a handle
  + ADVANCE supplies registry digest session identity sequence record digest and positive quota only
  + INSPECT returns the current handle and READ returns exact retained record JSON with digest
  + the process serializes changes so two equal-head advances admit exactly one successor
  + restart loses all sessions and no persistence claim is made

& [IdentityBoundary] prevents hidden authority
  + registry session record context checkpoint outcome and handle each have stable identities or content digests
  + every mutating command compare-and-sets the complete registry digest sequence and record digest
  + a terminal session cannot advance and a session identifier cannot be reused
  + retained state is readable exactly rather than represented only by a summary

& [TransportBoundary] keeps the attention control surface small
  + MCP schemas describe operation identities handles quotas and bounded JSON strings rather than recursively expanding the SOP runtime graph
  + OPEN and READ are host-oriented state transfer while ADVANCE and INSPECT are compact ordinary control operations
  + JSON strings remain strictly parsed typed and digest-bound inside Cantor and are not semantic authority by themselves

& [AuthorityBoundary] is dictated_source_only
  + canonical forms size limits digest rules concurrency lifecycle fault mapping tests documentation and acceptance require later SJS authority
  + no durable persistence authentication authorization provider invocation prompt insertion automatic tool choice network listener external effect production deployment or semantic truth is authorized
